# Mutation testing output. BOTH are required and BOTH hold full copies of source.
#
# `.stryker-tmp` is Stryker's sandbox: a complete copy of the project with one
# source file deliberately corrupted per mutant. A crashed run leaves it behind.
# `reports/` holds the HTML and JSON reports, which embed the mutated source too.
#
# ⚠️ This repository is PUBLIC. An un-ignored sandbox full of deliberately-broken
# source is one `git add -A` from being published — the same shape as `/coverage`
# above, with worse contents. The dot in `.stryker-tmp` protects nothing: it is not
# hidden from git, and ESLint 10's flat config traverses dot-directories (measured
# here 2026-08-24 — a planted copy of aggregation.ts was linted and added its own
# two complexity findings to the count).
/.stryker-tmp
/reports
.DS_Store
*.local
.claude/
.vercel
CLAUDE.md

# Architecture/design docs (not source code)
AHP_Doc*
AHP\ Doc*
*.docx
*.zip

# The incremental-build state file. `tsc -b` writes it even under `noEmit: true`,
# so unlike the patterns this block used to carry, it matches something: measured
# 2026-08-23, one file, 3181 bytes.
#
# Four sibling patterns were removed or re-filed here on the same date. The
# heading claimed one cause — "TypeScript build output (tsc -b artifacts)" — for
# five patterns with two different origins and only one live member, and the
# heading was the actual defect. `src/**/*.js` and `src/**/*.js.map` matched zero
# files and could not match any, because `noEmit: true` means `tsc` emits nothing;
# they were deleted. `vite.config.js*` moved to the Vite block below, where it
# belongs — see the note there.
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# Vite drops these at the project root when it bundles a TypeScript config, and
# leaves them behind if the process is killed. They sit beside vite.config.ts
# with a .mjs extension, which src/__tests__/copyright-headers.test.ts scopes as
# a root config file — an abandoned one turns `npm test` red for a reason the
# failure message cannot explain.
vite.config.*.timestamp-*
vitest.config.*.timestamp-*
#
# The two below are a vestige of an older Vite config-bundling mechanism, not of
# `tsc` — they sat under a "tsc -b artifacts" heading until 2026-08-23, which was
# never true of them. Measured zero matches, and inert for a second, independent
# reason: there is one `tsconfig.json` here, with no references and no composite,
# and `vite.config.ts` is not in its `include` set, so `tsc` never sees the file
# at all. Kept rather than deleted because an unmatched ignore line costs nothing,
# while a wrongly-deleted one puts a generated file untracked AND un-ignored in a
# public repository — the direction this repo has already been bitten in twice.
vite.config.js
vite.config.js.map

# Audit baseline for the dependency-upgrade campaign (local-only, not committed)
/.audit-baseline.json

# Working notes at the repository root — analyses, plans, critiques, briefings,
# orchestration prompts.
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS A SAFETY PATTERN, NOT TIDINESS. The source in this repository is
# public; the planning documents that accumulate in its root are not. One that is
# untracked but NOT ignored is a single `git add -A` away from a public commit.
# That has already happened twice in spert-forecaster, caught both times only
# because someone read the file list before pushing. A near-miss caught twice by
# attention is a hazard, not a track record.
#
# Measured 2026-08-11, before this block existed: 13 such files sat in this
# repository's root and `git check-ignore` matched exactly 0 of them.
#
# ⚠️ DENY-BY-DEFAULT WITH AN ALLOWLIST — deliberately NOT a vocabulary of
# filename patterns such as `/*-CRITIQUE-*.md` or `/*-PLAN-V*.md`. Measured
# across the suite: a vocabulary tuned to one repo's naming caught 4 of another
# repo's 108 documents, because the repos name things differently
# (`NAME-CRITIQUE-v2.md` versus `CRITIQUE_name.md`). An allowlist is complete by
# construction; a vocabulary is incomplete by construction.
#
# ⚠️ A vocabulary is also SELF-FLATTERING ON A MAC, which is the part that would
# not have been noticed. APFS is case-insensitive, so `/*-PLAN-V*.md` matches
# `...-plan-v3_1.md` here and matches nothing in CI. The same block measured 16
# on macOS and 4 under `git config core.ignorecase false`. The inflation happens
# where the pattern is authored; the deflation happens where it runs.
#
# The two failure directions are not symmetrical in a public repository:
#
#   - A doc wrongly ignored is visible on GitHub and fixed by one commit. It is
#     also not silent: `git add CONTRIBUTING.md` on an ignored path exits 1 and
#     prints a hint. Only `git add -A` passes over it quietly.
#   - A planning doc wrongly committed to a public repo is not recoverable. It is
#     in the history, cloneable and indexable.
#
# ⚠️ `.gitignore` has no effect on already-tracked files, so `/*.md` does not
# untrack anything listed below and never could. The allowlist is here so that
# creating a NEW SECURITY.md or CONTRIBUTING.md is still a plain `git add`, and
# so a reader can see at a glance what this repository means to publish.
#
# Derive the allowlist, never recall it:   git ls-files ':(glob)*.md'
/*.md
!/CHANGELOG.md
# Conventional root docs. README.md is tracked (added in e2e9e14); the other four are
# not present today and are listed so adding one needs no edit here.
!/README.md
!/ARCHITECTURE.md
!/SECURITY.md
!/CONTRIBUTING.md
!/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
